# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-terminal-encoding-fd-stream — -Eterminal must use the console API on Windows, not fd reopening
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: portability
#+DEPENDS: windows-access
#+BRANCH: feature-windows-terminal-encoding

* Context

=-Eterminal <enc>= reconfigures alfe's own =*standard-output*= /
=*error-output*= / =*standard-input*= to a chosen external-format. It is
implemented in =source/cli.lisp= (=%make-terminal-fd-stream= +
=apply-terminal-encoding=) by reopening OS descriptors 0/1/2 with
=sb-sys:make-fd-stream= and =setf='ing the stream globals.

* Problem

On Windows the reopen-by-fd approach is unreliable. =make-fd-stream= over
the inherited console descriptor *succeeds*, but the first *write* can fail
with =EBADF= (=Descripteur non valide=) under a non-console CI/msys shell.
That write happens later, in user code, past =apply-terminal-encoding='s
=handler-case=, so instead of the documented "degrade to a warning, never
abort" it aborted the whole run.

Surfaced on the native Windows runner as =CONFORMANCE-CORPUS-PASSES= failing
on the =cli-io-encoding= scenario (=--clautolisp -Eterminal utf-8 --dry-run
-x "(+ 1 2)"=), which runs in-process: the reopened FD-1 stream replaced the
harness's captured string stream and then EBADF'd.

* Stopgap (alfe 1.7.21)

=%make-terminal-fd-stream= now returns NIL on Windows (=#+(and sbcl (not
win32))=), so =-Eterminal= warns-and-keeps-default there rather than
crashing. POSIX SBCL is unchanged. The unit lane is green on all runners.

* Proposal

Implement terminal-encoding on Windows via the Console API rather than fd
reopening: set the active code page with =SetConsoleOutputCP= /
=SetConsoleCP= (CP_UTF8 = 65001) for the real console case, and for a
redirected/piped stdout wrap the stream with the requested external-format
without reopening the descriptor by number. Keep the "never abort, degrade
to a warning" contract: probe writability before installing any stream.

* Acceptance criteria

- =-Eterminal utf-8= on Windows makes alfe emit UTF-8 to a real console and
  to a redirected file, with no EBADF and no run abort.
- =cli-io-encoding= scenario passes on Windows while actually exercising the
  reconfiguration (not just the warn path).
- POSIX behaviour unchanged.
